# This file has moved — see the boot audit note below for why.
#
# Backend env template: apps/backend/.env.example  (copy to apps/backend/.env)
# Frontend env template: apps/web/.env.example      (copy to apps/web/.env.local)
#
# Why: this file used to live here, at the repo root, and README.md's
# quickstart used to say `cp ../../.env.example ../../.env` from within
# apps/backend/ — meaning the real .env ended up at the repo root too.
# That never actually worked: nothing in the backend loaded a .env file
# into process.env at all (found and fixed in main.ts — see its own
# comment on THE FIX), and even after fixing that, dotenv's default
# behavior reads .env from the current working directory, which is
# apps/backend/ whenever this app is actually started, not the repo
# root. Prisma's CLI has the same current-working-directory fallback and
# would have missed a repo-root .env the same way. Next.js's own env
# loading only ever looks in apps/web/, never a monorepo root, regardless
# of any of the above.
#
# Splitting into two per-app files, each co-located where its respective
# tool actually looks by default, fixes all of this with no code needed
# beyond the one dotenv loader now in main.ts.
